Presidential elections were held in [[Ghana]] on 3 November 1992. They were the first contested elections held in the country since [[1979 Ghanaian general election|1979]], and only the fourth contested elections of any sort since the country gained independence in 1957.

[[Jerry Rawlings]], who had led the country since taking power in a 1981 coup, had grudgingly agreed to hold multiparty elections earlier in the year.<ref name="NYT"/> Rawlings ran as the candidate of the Progressive Alliance, which included his [[National Democratic Congress (Ghana)|National Democratic Congress]], and won 58.4% of the vote, enough to win without the need for a runoff. Voter turnout was 50.2%.<ref>[[Dieter Nohlen]], Michael Krennerich & Bernhard Thibaut (1999) ''Elections in Africa: A data handbook'', p. 438 {{ISBN|0-19-829645-2}}</ref>

The opposition accused Rawlings and his supporters of engaging in massive fraud,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Lyons |first=Terrence |date=1997 |title=Ghana’s Encouraging Elections: A Major Step Forward |url=https://doi.org/10.1353/jod.1997.0019 |journal=Journal of Democracy |volume=8 |issue=2 |pages=65–77 |doi=10.1353/jod.1997.0019 |issn=1086-3214|url-access=subscription }}</ref> including ballot-box stuffing and altering results after they were certified. Nonetheless, international observers pronounced the elections free and fair.<ref name="NYT">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/11/06/world/ghana-s-leader-wins-election-as-president.html|title=Ghana's Leader Wins Election as President|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=6 November 1992}}</ref>

